  "label": "Learned Models For Protein Prediction",
  "related": [
    "language-agent-solution-fb69",
    "spatial-transcriptomics-7369",
    "targeting-design-7b7f"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [
    "systems"
  ],
  "slug": "gpu-accelerated-17d4",
  "summary": "Four April 2026 preprints probe when learned predictors transfer across shifted labels, targets and tasks, though their results remain provisional.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is that learned predictors are evaluated under explicit conditions: context, distribution shift, sample overlap and benchmark choice. A weak-label study on CRISPR-Cas13d RNA-seq guide efficacy reports that models transfer across cell lines but collapse under supervision drift in a non-IID benchmark. In target-conditional molecular property prediction across 10 protein families, FiLM fusion dominates, and context aids data-scarce settings. Activity cliff prediction over 25M matched molecular pairs from 50 ChEMBL targets finds that an 11-feature 3D pharmacophore model outperforms scaffold-size baselines. Gradient-based task affinity estimation requires over 40% sample overlap to recover pathways, and MoleculeNet and TDC fall short, which accounts for inconsistent multi-task learning. What is new is the quantification of these conditions rather than architecture alone. All four items are preprints, so their results remain provisional.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-09"
}
